treekipedia:AngMaFaFgCx14878-00 tkp:culturalSignificance "Traditional food use: Acorns edible after leaching tannins; used as food by indigenous peoples of the region. Traditional methods: Acorns buried in boggy ground or soaked in running water to remove tannins. Medicinal: Oak bark and galls used traditionally as astringent, antibacterial treatments for diarrhea, dysentery, and external wounds. Modern significance: Native plant for xeriscaping and habitat restoration in southwestern US." .
