treekipedia:AngMaMaMlVc33502-00 tkp:culturalSignificance "Sacred tree in many African cultures; believed to house spirits and connect physical and spiritual worlds. Mythology: Gods planted trees upside-down as punishment for pride (explaining root-like branches). Hausa tradition: djinns (spirits) dwell inside; children warned to avoid trees at dusk. Community role: Village meeting places; traditional courts convene under shade; elders share wisdom and stories. Ritual use: Libations poured, prayers offered at trees; burial sites for griots (storytellers) in Senegal. Symbol of: strength, resilience, longevity, community, ancestral connection. Called Tree of Life for multiple uses. Featured in African art, proverbs, and oral traditions across continent." .
